Game notes:
This one was in early November, so I remember a lot more of it. I was
making a more conscious effort for good inventory management,
especially since I didn't get a sack until well after Minetown (no
tool shop generated, and Izzy had no magic lamps), and didn't get a
(BoH until I could wish for one. The turn count is in large part due
to much running back and forth between stashes in the midgame, and a
lot of altar camping trying to get artifacts. For the record, I got
gifted with Snickersnee (which I lost track of), Ogresmasher (Worst.
Gift. Ever.), Sunsword (kept it for the light source, if I had to burn
my magic lamp wish), Frosty (finally!), and Werebane (first silver
saber -- wasn't going to expend the effort trying for Grayswandir at
this point with this many artifacts in play).

My last Samurai to make the Quest got bisected. (I think the last time
I made the Quest as a Sam was in 3.1.3, before Ashikaga could wield
the Tsurugi.) So this time I went in with a potion of polymorph, tins
of chameleon and doppelganger, and wearing dragon scale mail. (Blue --
that's the best that Ludios could serve up.) Facing Ashikaga, I took
off my cloak, poly'd into a dragon, and went after him...and reverted
to human after two turns of melee thanks to "dying". So at this point,
I went Indiana Jones on him and pulled out my .455 revolver...erm,
wand of death, and shot him rather than going through the whole
swordfight routine.

Between the Quest and the start of the trip through Gehennom was the
worst part of the game. I had already decided to go polyitemless
unless it became absolutely impossible to proceed without doing so.
Inconvenience does not equal impossible, though, so I managed to put
together my AK the hard way. The relative lack of enchantment on my
armour and weapons does speak to the refusal to polypile. I also was
reluctant to use magic lamps for wishes for "necessary" stuff, since
the wish isn't a sure guarantee. Still, survival wasn't terribly
difficult. Going through with a less than ideal kit does train you for
paying more attention to what you're doing, of course. Gehennom was
actually a refreshing change from what I had been going through up to
that point...and that's not a comment you'll hear in most APs.

I almost forgot to take down Vlad before going after Rodney. The
stairs to Vlad's were on Orcus's level. (Didn't someone just do YAAP
with this same problem?)

Near the bottom of Gehennom, I found myself with a b?SoTaming and a
purple worm to use it on, and so I decided to have some fun. I did the
best I could without having a stethoscope, and I almost got the worm
up to Rodney, but it got killed one level below. Hence my level of
annoyance was such that I tinned up his corpse after the first time I
killed him.

Again because of my refusal to polypile and a lack of magic markers, I
went into the Planes with only one ?SoGD. I saved that one for Water.
It wasn't horribly tough to find the Portals "the hard way", and since
this had never been any attempt at a speed run, I didn't care that it
took me a while.

As I said above, my guardian Angel got swallowed by a purple worm.
Karma is a bitch. I had also forgotten about Death's ability to drain
your maxHP, and it didn't help that I had to go through all 3 branches
of Astral. Still, a win's a win.

  Llina, drowned in a moat by a kraken.


